# Request a twin mode transition (ADR-0011).
#
# A service rather than an action because the request is bounded: it either is
# permitted now or it is not. The transition itself is observable on
# /cite/twin/mode, which is where a caller watches it complete.
#
# SIM -> REAL, entry to CLOSED_LOOP, and entry to VIRTUAL_LEAD against a real far
# side are the three transitions that matter most. The criterion the three share is
# stated once, in docs/architecture/cross-cutting-safety.md: a transition qualifies
# when it places physical actuation under an authority that was not previously
# commanding it. VIRTUAL_LEAD meets it with nothing interposed — it is CLOSED_LOOP
# minus the validation gate, aimed at the same arm (ADR-0041 Decision 2).
#
# THOSE THREE ARE NOT THE GATE, AND READING THEM AS THE GATE COST THIS PROJECT A
# BYPASS. They are the three that matter most, which is a statement about
# emphasis; the gate is the criterion, and the criterion decides every mode.
# VALIDATED also dispatches an operator goal to both sides — by byte-identical
# code to VIRTUAL_LEAD's — and it is not in the list of three, so until
# 2026-08-31 SetMode(VIRTUAL_LEAD) was refused SAFETY_BLOCKED on a plan with a
# real far side while SetMode(VALIDATED) was accepted with no gate and no
# `force`, after which a goal reached the physical arm. Never transcribe this
# list into code.
#
# Whether entering VIRTUAL_LEAD can move anything physical is a PER-ASSET fact.
# Where that asset's far side is a simulated counterpart, entering the mode moves
# nothing physical for that asset. This request is not always per-asset: TwinMode
# carries asset_id with "empty for facility-wide", and charter §8 states the
# system runs with one physical arm and two simulated ones. So a facility-wide
# SetMode(VIRTUAL_LEAD) is dangerous if ANY single asset's far side is real, and
# two assets answering "simulated" is not an answer for the third.
#
# What enforces the hardware opt-in today, stated exactly, because this file used
# to state a guarantee that no code provided:
#
#   * Bring-up refuses to start a zone whose plan names a hardware backend unless
#     CITE_ALLOW_HARDWARE=1 is set — cite_bringup/plan.py, require_hardware_opt_in.
#   * ./scripts/enter hardware refuses the same way, at the shell boundary.
#
# Both bind at BRING-UP and not at the transition. What they buy is that the stack
# could not have started with a physical backend; neither is a per-command refusal,
# so neither refuses any transition this service carries. Do not read the list above
# as saying these three are gated at the point of transition — they are not.
#
# What enforces it on this service, as of the L5 mode server: a transition that
# places physical actuation under an authority that was not previously commanding
# it calls `cite_bringup.plan.require_hardware_opt_in` — the SAME check, at the
# transition rather than only at bring-up — and refuses with SAFETY_BLOCKED when
# it raises. `force` does not skip it.
#
# WHICH TRANSITIONS THOSE ARE IS COMPUTED AND NOT LISTED, which is the whole of
# the correction above. `cite_twin` asks two questions of data: which sides the
# requested mode commands — `cite_twin.routing.commanded_sides`, read off
# TwinMode.msg's own per-mode comment, and a superset of the sides L5 dispatches
# a goal to — and whether any of those sides, for any asset in scope, loads a
# backend that is not a simulation. So the answer on a cell with a physical far
# side is every mode but SIM, including VALIDATED and including SHADOW, and a
# seventh mode is decided by the same two questions rather than by whether
# somebody remembered to add it.
#
# A facility-wide request resolves the backend of EVERY asset before it decides,
# because two assets answering "simulated" is not an answer for the third.
#
# On a deployment where no side is physical, no mode is gated and the response
# says so: there is nothing there for a mode to place under a new authority, and
# the check is plan-scoped, so it could not have refused one anyway.
#
# What that still is not: the safety layer. It is one refusal in one server, and
# a hardware path that is opted into is not thereby supervised (P7).
#
# SERVICE below is the ONE place this name is written, for the same reason
# ResetStation.srv carries its own.

string SERVICE="/cite/twin/set_mode"

uint8 mode                  # one of TwinMode.MODE_*
string reason               # recorded; required, so every transition has a why
bool force                  # skip non-safety preconditions. Never skips a safety
                            # check — no value of this field can do that, and
                            # two refusals are structurally out of its reach:
                            # the hardware gate above, and the refusal of a
                            # transition taken while goals L5 dispatched are
                            # still running. The second is not decoration — a
                            # mode published ahead of the cell says "physical
                            # idle" while an arm is moving, and forcing it would
                            # be forcing that statement onto every reader of
                            # /cite/twin/mode.
---
bool accepted
ResultCode result
uint8 current_mode          # the mode in force after this call, accepted or not
